    "hypothesis = '''Between 1990 and 1996, wealth inequality rose modestly within both white females and white males in the NLS 1979 cohort (n = 3,630 white females, 3,469 white males, with zero missing values in either wealth year). On the raw data, the headline Gini coefficient moved from 0.776 (95% bootstrap CI 0.758-0.793) to 0.782 (95% CI 0.769-0.794) for white females (point-estimate change +0.006; within-resampling 95% CI on the change -0.015 to +0.024), and from 0.792 (95% CI 0.775-0.807) to 0.798 (95% CI 0.785-0.809) for white males (point-estimate change +0.006; within-resampling 95% CI on the change -0.010 to +0.024). The direction of rising inequality is corroborated by a widening of the bulk-of-distribution metrics: standard deviation rose by $34,875 for white females and $72,747 for white males; the interquartile range rose by $43,875 (+40%) and $56,655 (+62%) respectively. The top-decile wealth share, in contrast, held essentially flat in both groups (change +0.000 for females; -0.002 for males), indicating that the 1990 -> 1996 increase in overall dispersion was driven by widening of the middle of the distribution (IQR, CV) rather than further concentration at the very top over this short window. Secondary: white males remained slightly more unequal than white females in both years (dGini(M-F) +0.016 in 1990 and +0.016 in 1996); the male-female Gini gap did not change materially, so both groups show comparable upward drift in within-group inequality. Findings are robust to (a) winsorization at the 1st/99th percentile, where the Gini still rises for both groups (0.736->0.769 for females and 0.766->0.785 for males), and (b) restricting to ever_jailed = 0, which removes only 12 of 3,630 white females and 57 of 3,469 white males; the ever_jailed = 1 subgroup is too small (12 F + 57 M) for a stratified comparison, and is reported only via the ever_jailed = 0 robustness variant.'''\n",
